\section{Draw Three Boundaries Around a Module}

Suppose a renderer lets its clients reserve image tiles. Its module
\texttt{Tiles} exports an opaque \texttt{Pool}, a constructor, and
\texttt{reserve(Pool, Size)}. A caller must know the signature of
\texttt{reserve}, and the ABI must tell it how to pass a pool handle.
Neither obligation implies that the caller should know the allocator's
free-list layout. Likewise the implementation might call a helper
with external linkage from another object file, without admitting
that helper into the source-language public interface.

Distinguish three audiences. The author of a module sees private
definitions; the importer sees the language-level interface; the
object linker sees the symbols and relocations it must process
\cite{levine2000linkers}. All three may use different names for the
same function. A source-private item can have a linker symbol because
two implementation objects share it. A public type can have no linker
symbol at all. Putting language visibility in an object symbol flag
alone cannot express these distinctions.
The distinction also applies when a language imports a native
function: finding the address does not check the type or the
ownership and calling protocol that the wrapper promises
(\Cref{ch:ext-396}).

\section{Decide What an Importer Must Know}

An interface file is a compact, typed description of the external
contract, not an edited copy of the source. A useful starting point
is a stable definition identity per declaration, together with the
kind of declaration, type, constraints, visibility, and any facts a
consumer may embed in its own object. Ada illustrates the separation
by allowing specification and body to be compiled as distinct units.
An implementation is checked against its specification; a client is
checked against the specification instead of the implementation.
\Cref{fig:visual-397} distinguishes this type-checking boundary from
the later link.

