The toy compiler promises that a pool is passed as one opaque
handle. Changing the body from a list to a bitmap is compatible
with this promise. Changing the constructor from one argument to
two is not. A less obvious example is \texttt{TileSize}, defined as
a compile-time constant: a switch or an array bound in a client may
already contain its numerical value. Its value belongs to the
interface even if no relocation ever refers to it.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Declaration & Importer records & Object consequence \\
\hline
Opaque \texttt{Pool} & identity, handle ABI & no exposed field offsets \\
\texttt{reserve} & type, calling convention & external call relocation \\
\texttt{TileSize} & type and value & immediate or array extent \\
Private free list & nothing & local data or hidden symbol \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

An opaque type passed by value is a different design. Even if field
names are hidden, size, alignment, copy rules, and destruction can
affect the caller. Either publish the necessary layout facts or choose
an ABI that keeps the storage behind an indirection. Hiding the
names while silently exposing the layout defeats implementation
independence, although it still prevents source-level field access.

