\section{Build and Validate an Interface Artifact}

\begin{verbatim}
compile_unit(spec, body, resolved_imports, target):
    imported := load_interfaces(resolved_imports, target)
    promised := check_declarations(spec, imported)
    public := normalize_external_contract(promised, target)
    require_no_unnameable_public_types(public)
    checked := check_body(body, imported, promised)
    require_all_promises_implemented(promised, checked)
    fingerprint := hash(format_revision, target.abi, public)
    object := emit_object(checked, imported)
    object.provides := (public.module_id, fingerprint)
    object.requires := fingerprints_consumed_during_emission()
    publish_together(public, fingerprint, object)

check_link_inputs(objects):
    providers := index_unique_providers(objects)
    for obj in objects:
        for (module_id, expected) in obj.requires:
            actual := providers[module_id].interface_fingerprint
            if actual != expected:
                error("rebuild consumer", obj, module_id)
    resolve_relocations(objects)
\end{verbatim}

Here fingerprint matching is part of the build driver, not a claim
about what a conventional linker understands. The driver must reject
a missing provider distinctly from a fingerprint mismatch. The
interface serializer should be stable across irrelevant source
edits: fresh type variables need canonical numbering, unordered
maps need a canonical encoding, and private source locations need
not affect the external fingerprint. On the other hand, the
serializer must preserve semantically meaningful order, such as the
order of public fields if it controls offsets.

\section{Track Extra Promises Introduced by Optimization}

Imagine the renderer inlines \texttt{reserve} into a client when the
implementation is available. A change to the free list may then
leave the source contract untouched while invalidating the inlined
instructions in that client. Record a separate body dependency for
cross-module inlining. A compiler that emits specialization templates
must handle those similarly. Do not include every private routine in
every client's fingerprint, and do not pretend a hash of signatures
validates code that has copied implementation details. A conservative
build system can rebuild more than required; an unsound one can keep
an old instruction sequence in a new executable.

Test independence by building a consumer once and relinking it
against a body-only change, with intermodule optimization disabled.
Then test the reverse: arrange for a constant or calling convention
to change under the same symbol spelling and verify that the driver
rejects the old consumer. Repeat with an inlining build and require
the extra dependency to invalidate it. Finally compile an unrelated
consumer that tries to name the private helper and insist on a
front-end diagnostic even if a matching symbol appears in an object
dump. These are separate checks of separate boundaries.

\section{Handle a Change That Crosses Only One Boundary}

Suppose version A lets clients construct an alignment of 32 bytes
by reading the public constant \texttt{TileSize}. Version B raises
it to 64, but retains the same handle and routine signatures.
The module can still process a request from any object that obeys
the binary calling convention. That alone does not make the
mixture correct: the contract no longer says that a caller's
32-byte scratch area is sufficient. Linking the two objects
without checking their interface fingerprints hides the mismatch
until some rendering branch uses the old scratch area.

To keep binary clients, redesign this promise intentionally.
Provide a query whose result is passed to the allocation routine,
accept both alignments for a documented compatibility period,
or version the entry point. A query is not equivalent to a constant
in a language that permits it in type-level array extents. In
that case, retaining the old constant as a supported compatibility
surface or deliberately requiring recompilation may be a better
choice than replacing it with a runtime operation.

\begin{verbatim}
event       interface hash    symbol reserve     old consumer
edit list   unchanged         unchanged          usable
change 32   changed           unchanged          reject at build
rename ABI  changed           changed            reject at build
new helper  unchanged         unchanged          usable
\end{verbatim}

This trace assumes the normalized interface also encodes all
ABI-relevant names or version tags; renaming a linker name alone
must either change the recorded ABI contract or be covered by an
alias recognized by the linker. During a parallel build, a client
must not see a new interface published alongside an old object,
even if both files are individually well formed. One implementation
publishes a generation directory and atomically switches a manifest
pointer. Consumers pin that generation while they read. Besides
resolving symbols, the build then guarantees that the interfaces
describing the call and the objects providing its implementation
came from compatible compilations.
Add a fixture that attempts to mix artifacts from two generations
of an otherwise unchanged module. If both fingerprints match,
the mix is intentionally legal under the interface policy; if an
embedded layout changes, repeat and require rejection.
